 2006)\\n......................................................................................................................................... 12\\n\\nFigure 2.3: The first-generation prototype. (Subrahmanian and Ehmann 2002) ................. 13\\n\\nFigure 2.4: Constructed 5-axis milling machine (Bang et al. 2005) ...................................... 13\\n\\nFigure 2.5: Components of the machining micro-factory. (Okazaki et al., 2004) .................. 14\\n\\nFigure 2.6: Scaling effect on tool geometry and wear (work material: 40CrMnMo7, 50 HRC,\\nVc =200/100 m/min, ap = ae = 0.04 x d, fz = 0.01 x d, z= 2, down milling, tool material:\\ncemented carbide with TiAlN coating. (Dornfeld et al., 2006) ............................................. 16\\n\\nFigure 2.7: Demonstrates tool wear occurring during the micro-milling of hardened steel as a\\nfunction of the engagement length and tool diameter. (Dornfeld et al., 2006) ..................... 16\\n\\nFigure 2.8: Scanning electron micrograph (SEM) of micro-cutting tools. (Adams et al., 2001)\\n......................................................................................................................................... 16\\n\\nFigure 2.9: Cutting force variation corresponding with the grain boundary of Al alloy (grain\\nboundaries can be seen in magnified image in A and B). (Dornfeld et al., 2006) .................. 18\\n\\nFigure 2.10: Schematic of the effect of the minimum chip thickness (Chae et al., 2006). ...... 20\\n\\nFigure 2.11: Apparent critical depth of cut versus feed: (100) germanium and silicon with -\\n30 rake and 6 clearance angles (Dornfeld et al., 2006). ..................................................... 22\\n\\fFigure 2.12. a) An exit burr b) Categorization of burr types. (Lee and Dornfeld 2002) ........ 23\\n\\nFigure 2.13: a) Microdrilling burr formation (250 m) (Min et al., 2006) ............................. 24\\n\\nFigure 2.14: Photos of burrs (100x) in micro-machining: (a)